ield vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/b7/b73b05cb7f58bcce1d684c7f19c391ecd2033eb4c47c59daf896546956a9d42f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/b8/b8b4cae5128667b4a75097540bddc8dbb853744aee65e96204cf630ccb30fc68.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387311 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1da3c3d0-ffca-58b0-9973-21ca78efe966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/93/9355e5a989137d0d74ee803bf0fb0248fc68e4df4aa1843afede93d1bb506a45.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387313} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6c888d-3419-51b8-8e63-8b6f80586e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/00/00b722a09fef4428d17fc7ff6a301b3860e798b20c44126b63cd381ad2884398.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387314} {:answer/id #uuid "522cd20b-1e88-577f-9897-b7d808dced3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ce/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387315} {:answer/id #uuid "7370d0a5-e8d6-5b38-af6b-5d647d33fadb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8e/8e30bba74fad98713f891950fd052d2ddd2cb2dc349bd9bcb7efe3333b7203dd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387316} {:answer/id #uuid "da9ecd53-f00c-59aa-9818-8a0bb184f65d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/14/14c453b9fcf4670a2356b730253dd7f411b989abde303f059ebb5a9d559a4eff.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387317}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387315} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c14dfe7-e6c0-5526-b094-6b4a396050e0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "70739974-680b-512e-bb5a-c7c80411f351" :question/math-academy-id "q-122838" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f7dfad4e44184f68de36d455e99895fce8c879d4b053c06aa5805c423bdb1bfc.png)\n\nThe gradient vector field of the function $f(x,y)$ is shown above. Which of the following could be a contour plot of $f(x,y)?$" :question/worked-solution "The gradient field vectors of a function $f(x,y)$ always lie perpendicular to its level curves.\n\nObserve that the given field vectors are perpendicular to the level curves in the following picture.\n\n![](images/9f/9f22df07aac0933106381dce4571e674d656428bf7a976e16bed6d46898f814f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](images/ce/cecc7f28c20523aac9f153022d64da4233ce995e05df3b3e2886b7fb3b20d089.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387367 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9653f49-1a01-5acd-8ce6-f3cd8095fdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{7t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387369} {:answer/id #uuid "2d3322db-d223-55b3-b7dc-f9fd758349fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{3t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387370} {:answer/id #uuid "e3c61a6c-a91d-50a4-a701-bf4e530a3094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387371} {:answer/id #uuid "d4be85c4-9c1a-5af1-b31a-957bb0b77474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387372} {:answer/id #uuid "95f050db-1db6-5212-8a8b-3de82028eac7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{7t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387372} :answer-field/id #uuid "e77fad7f-b6e3-5827-9df5-363a99a7be9d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b2a142a9-d042-56b2-ba86-b30a7fb0dfd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-123283" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y},$ traversed along the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,0)$ to $(-3,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=0$ and $b=-3\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =0+t\\,(b-0) \\\\  & =t(b) \\\\  & =t(-3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-3t\\,i+4t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-3t$ and $y=4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)j \\\\  & =-3\\,i+4\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={e}^{x+y}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={e}^{-3t+4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\cdot 5\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387381 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e2e9acc-3c64-51a7-9ab5-d350f9449ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387383} {:answer/id #uuid "89ac3f7c-fb26-5086-aea8-2db3445e4653" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387384} {:answer/id #uuid "ef97d2f5-fde9-5e22-82b7-c60bd3fea467" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{t-1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387385} {:answer/id #uuid "ec794748-a3c8-58b6-8929-a9cf739d2848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{5t-3}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387386} {:answer/id #uuid "26182461-9d9e-569f-b647-6e1cb1f93a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387387}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387387} :answer-field/id #uuid "36154aca-9593-55ae-8c89-27fef1f72807" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "963025de-8b37-535e-a08b-0b16983c48ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-123387" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the line integral of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z},$ traversed along the line segment from $(3,-2,3)$ to $(5,-1,7)?$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(3,-2,3)$ to $(5,-1,7).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=3i-2\\,j+3k$ and $b=5\\,i-j+7\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(3i-2\\,j+3k)+t[(5\\,i-j+7\\,k)-(3i-2\\,j+3k)] \\\\  & =(3i-2\\,j+3k)+t(2i+\\,j+4k) \\\\  & =(2t+3)\\,i+(t-2)\\,j+(4t+3)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t+3,y=t-2,$ and $z=4t+3$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+3)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-2)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t+3)k \\\\  & =2i+j+4\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{2x+y-z}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\sqrt{2(2t+3)+(t-2)-(4t+3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\cdot \\sqrt{21}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{21}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66dc688f-81a1-5bab-892b-ab84541785d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387472} {:answer/id #uuid "d1cf7a39-05f3-5b46-9687-4304489da63d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387473} {:answer/id #uuid "b3aaa33f-356e-5b31-9322-7fc460783b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387474} {:answer/id #uuid "6904468a-7356-52cb-8fc4-603b5ee7fd3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387475} {:answer/id #uuid "b3bac43c-45b2-592b-a306-006e20a13c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387476}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387473} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a48ec71-cabe-53f3-8125-9985126072d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "76ca20f1-204f-59f9-8369-ed13b85fecd2" :question/math-academy-id "q-124218" :question/problem "![](images/47/4727c988042bd8d63af370c507db8988b3024cf7e75c86d40bc70cdcfd744873.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}y{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot {e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{e}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387477 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b65a2499-03be-5905-9103-3bc639070075" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387479} {:answer/id #uuid "02e5a48e-caff-5b56-923a-edebcab3b203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387480} {:answer/id #uuid "ef19f835-6146-5aac-b21a-93caea15dc24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387481} {:answer/id #uuid "043ccaf9-23b9-56e1-8926-73f293a9c821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387482} {:answer/id #uuid "088e8adc-3efa-55dc-b823-271ee24d9d3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387483}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387483} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a794707-9bf7-53f4-9c51-8d9a3d67187e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6572425b-1f69-589f-ade1-f4702feda38e" :question/math-academy-id "q-124221" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9babf4c2ec631b3b872760261a46c3552cb93b93df90515ff0605c15043841ca.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(-4,0)$ to the point $(4,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }4(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387484 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a8d9450-4800-5112-a544-9e325560e579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387486} {:answer/id #uuid "047d0818-f7ba-51e2-b160-54925fc6a14f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387487} {:answer/id #uuid "029046db-d3c3-5031-8d7b-336fa4ba1219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387488} {:answer/id #uuid "2fdec4ad-6b88-5993-8466-125c2cf0469c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}{sin}^{2}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387489} {:answer/id #uuid "9cb35e9d-0c57-5ee2-93e2-209a367b2cb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387490}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387486} :answer-field/id #uuid "09c0fb83-a54b-597a-9d51-eaad92361b4c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387485}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "48afea1b-7419-5b27-97ac-ca8861895f05" :question/math-academy-id "q-124277" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f532544febd248846eede65941f4617d325d0f91fec291d2679ba28abb2fb8fc.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $2,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387491 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cc88e3d-a852-5587-8a01-ed034f9f4385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387493} {:answer/id #uuid "0354e92a-880d-5b37-9463-ad3a8ea451ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387494} {:answer/id #uuid "05b857f2-d2e7-58cf-af80-9a20296d1870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387495} {:answer/id #uuid "5303fcf8-09a5-5b94-af0f-54b031ab7e05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387496} {:answer/id #uuid "29b535f4-dc26-5f10-9f77-d35c710a0661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387495} :answer-field/id #uuid "1af7ee7b-5d10-5025-8624-92a4d018ecb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e84b6989-0b06-5564-bfd3-0660ddb2a037" :question/math-academy-id "q-124293" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d02a56e1f927ccf8bd76a6fcc46dec61259649176695912865dd050657ce7f0.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along a quarter of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(0,1)$ to the point $(-2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387498 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bf68642-1bc2-5ac7-9a05-766d932162e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387500} {:answer/id #uuid "f72d462d-8b4a-5ce9-af5e-b55e5d4bd6d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }(3{sin}^{2}⁡t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387501} {:answer/id #uuid "64c3b71b-6781-591c-9b16-935e82c7d8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t+3){sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387502} {:answer/id #uuid "7e3ffd3c-9264-5a32-8db9-c3420cd55260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387503} {:answer/id #uuid "38807c4a-be0d-5f98-b548-41b11aa72f13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t+3){cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387504}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387503} :answer-field/id #uuid "164502c8-6757-5ba5-b73c-b45e5adc8b09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d9bcf07-4605-5aed-9b42-699d630fbd7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-124298" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd1956c8291f1bcecd7d37082110861ed7265fa40f7f85cbc69221cfdc5bea2d.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the ellipse $16{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=64,$ traversed from the point $(-2,0)$ to the point $(2,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le 2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =12{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡t+12({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{2}⁡t+12 \\\\  & =4({sin}^{2}⁡t+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }4({sin}^{2}⁡t+3)\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }({sin}^{2}⁡t+3)\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387533 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2f1d0a0-f937-5c5a-a906-b8d8c3476536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387535} {:answer/id #uuid "b7352c23-717f-5780-b0d0-69655ec1cf5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387536} {:answer/id #uuid "aa9c4e1a-9de0-5b54-9a84-bd61bf918f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387537} {:answer/id #uuid "8321670f-4321-5b31-a978-0d03d440b8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387538} {:answer/id #uuid "6b7e8a42-2b6d-59b6-8e6b-34f626db24de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387537} :answer-field/id #uuid "46e3edf1-c65d-5b40-9b5a-4947c9652286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387534}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "35a5240c-df73-5f77-b045-605d4d50b078" :question/math-academy-id "q-124651" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx+xy\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=4$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $1,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,dx+xy\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+xy\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}({x}^{2}\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+xy\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}-8{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387540 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e75bdd4-9ffa-598b-ba2b-3e4718966455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387542} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b5e560-c25e-5180-91e9-4dd26cebdf25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387543} {:answer/id #uuid "9e5e8954-0ec8-5e2c-87e8-00cd2ba07083" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387544} {:answer/id #uuid "e56c1b23-1bc1-5e37-920c-d6b5c4c83b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+1\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387545} {:answer/id #uuid "96172456-0f12-5f8e-95b8-f6b631ca3655" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387546}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387546} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f4769e4-af1d-5493-a3fe-c583f362931e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c8b59c59-a390-5b22-8421-70bf95742c06" :question/math-academy-id "q-124653" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}({e}^{x}+y)\\,dx-x\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=3$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(0,\\sqrt{3})$ to the point $B(-\\sqrt{3},0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is a circle of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({e}^{x}+y)\\,dx-x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({e}^{x}+y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }(({e}^{x}+y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}-x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }({e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-3{sin}^{2}⁡t-3{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}-3\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+3\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57571e56-49bb-5855-a1ea-93ab592af29a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6{sin}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387549} {:answer/id #uuid "dee85688-f7f4-5420-abcb-2c8946c6128c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387550} {:answer/id #uuid "581fc888-b8c4-5b98-a662-97d6df2c1c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387551} {:answer/id #uuid "0fa25052-4bcd-5645-84a9-cb44c291ba75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387552} {:answer/id #uuid "179cd5d2-4e71-5c6d-adbe-d8d33387b74f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}3-{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387553}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387551} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab73ef72-cd0a-53ca-954d-b2e9e89157ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387548}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4adcf258-fc9a-5300-865b-c8efb5f76002" :question/math-academy-id "q-124655" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(x-2y)\\,dx+2x\\,dy,\n$$ where $C$ is part of the ellipse $9{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ traversed in the counterclockwise direction from the point $A(0,3)$ to the point $B(0,-3).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $3,$ respectively, it can be parametrized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =x(t)\\,i+y(t)\\,j=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(x-2y)\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(x-2y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+2x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}((x-2y)\\,\\frac{dx}{\\text{d}t}+2x\\,\\frac{dy}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t))\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{sin}^{2}⁡t+6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}6-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a901625-a97b-587a-a229-db393b0faa09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{4}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387631} {:answer/id #uuid "65bb9729-0f4b-55d2-a79a-45bfa7c48b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2x\\,i+j+3{z}^{2}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387632} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3a5bf0-63dc-54c4-b414-83caa0016fee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\,i+y\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387633} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d415eb-b557-5d40-a52e-e5602f1903e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∇({x}^{2}+y+{z}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387634} {:answer/id #uuid "95642c53-04e5-595d-a5b9-d72091dfb374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "∇(2x+3{z}^{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387635}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387633} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6079ffa-a6d7-5d2d-8433-f9ab6491cabd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a8e91f33-d259-5586-a72c-0ad1065dc0eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-125427" :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+y\\,\\text{d}y+{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z\n$$ is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$ for some piecewise smooth curve $C,$ then $F(x,y,z)=$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y+R\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nIn our case, $P={x}^{2},Q=y,R={z}^{3}.$ Therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}({x}^{2}\\,i+y\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k)\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+y\\,\\text{d}y+{z}^{3}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $F={x}^{2}\\,i+y\\,j+{z}^{3}\\,k.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8440b232-2d79-53cd-bad6-36fe06f0e97a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388078} {:answer/id #uuid "fec3679d-d002-508a-b44e-896ae847115f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388079} {:answer/id #uuid "d41769d3-a85e-5a1d-b0d2-621fb6f6adf3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388080} {:answer/id #uuid "8d510eb4-4bca-58e9-bca7-c33fc8cc7f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388081} {:answer/id #uuid "112648f6-f22c-57a0-8ebb-0ebd53decbdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388082} :answer-field/id #uuid "b68b09e4-9252-5d68-ab26-fe3cc2a6e217" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7c1bed00-c1da-539a-bd7b-b11b7bf684ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-130094" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-x+y,$ find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-x+y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =({y}^{2}-1)\\text{ }i+(2xy+1)\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(1,-1) & =((-1)^{2}-1)\\,i+(2(1)(-1)+1)\\,j \\\\  & =0\\,i-j \\\\  & =⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-(-1)⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59f4e581-a00c-5132-927c-1585f2f16335" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388085} {:answer/id #uuid "a25cb316-03e0-5b80-b36c-bf23e9ecd400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{7}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388086} {:answer/id #uuid "7ce509f6-676c-5142-b2e8-6b75044a4ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388087} {:answer/id #uuid "56675fa5-8d6f-5214-9ce9-5265d8d72af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(-1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388088} {:answer/id #uuid "5bff0bff-8fcd-5363-b279-0ef233e06a17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388089}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388086} :answer-field/id #uuid "368f7e81-de7f-51a5-98fc-f718da4aa1b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f930a915-2d68-50d6-9185-efb4889fde3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-130209" :question/problem "At which point on the curve $2{x}^{2}=y+1$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i+j?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2} & =y+1 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}-y & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}-y,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-y)\\,i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-y)\\,j \\\\  & =-i-4x\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i+j,$ we must have $t\\cdot (i+j)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot (i+j) & =0 \\\\ (-i-4x\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) & =0 \\\\ -1-4x & =0 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}$ into the equation of our curve gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2} & =y+1 \\\\ 2{\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2} & =y+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & =y+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the corresponding point is $(-\\frac{1}{4},-\\frac{7}{8}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389181 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "659a692b-2aae-5acc-a235-560ceb57bafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389183} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c0fc1d-78bf-56a3-bddf-42bd659ad7fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/30/3033f0ae73e3f484fc5d3d65e388062feba1824fb3da9971d560cf2f518f49bb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389184} {:answer/id #uuid "404681f6-e3e2-5a85-a1a6-1e1e580bf865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d5/d5889bd5e8d537757de25a7b1a868ff8a903da14c9088c5f8de5a70d8b078552.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389185} {:answer/id #uuid "121673a3-bbfa-573d-a9d1-f20fe0e8e765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/cb/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389186} {:answer/id #uuid "e0e134b4-7103-5125-b7fc-086313478261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b0/b0171dfd42295e39cd45186e8edba3a5105fbd1bd02a1621223091de75815f61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389187}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389186} :answer-field/id #uuid "3825278d-de78-5afb-82f2-adfd041f336b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c2f0da55-1926-59a4-96c8-e68855af7a42" :question/math-academy-id "q-161837" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3}+v \\\\ uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{u}^{2} & 1 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={u}^{2}\\cdot u-v\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={u}^{3}-v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}-v & <0 \\\\ v & >{u}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv={u}^{3}\n$$\n\ndefines a cubic function, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/cb/cb5b22ff46569b231d122c2ed11bd412907f36271a033d86769435adf9fe48c7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389188 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "773f7224-683a-5657-93fd-0b49bba9ddc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389190} {:answer/id #uuid "3223453f-3db3-5cf7-9170-0776a21f7b7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8b/8b35fbafaf8d121bceef7a0763253df075fe4fe85eb3c992f95c59d05f986c67.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389191} {:answer/id #uuid "d90c24c3-b6bf-58dc-bb07-e1245dcf6b0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/09/09f7f36ecc2197cc95e1878fa22ac22367978809a5241f09886a7eb2fab071f3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389192} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae8e4b1-ec09-543b-b30d-54e69ad80dcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/10/105e6e8a010b7b9001f75cf17e11cbbc1e031d83cbc2ddfafc3595c745106f3f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389193} {:answer/id #uuid "682bc6d2-be17-5ccb-842c-4b844ea68ed2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/09/090bab0ac1c6356010d83a6ad54c59b53b7dd12062fb5e331a936ce3f3a1b411.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389194}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389191} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bd81eba-4d1a-5e26-b6d5-47144737edcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "99972da5-e4e5-5505-b6d1-ad2a467bcb50" :question/math-academy-id "q-161839" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ uv+1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}-v \\\\ uv+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & -1 \\\\ v & u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot u-v\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2{u}^{2}+v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{u}^{2}+v & >0 \\\\ v & >-2{u}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-2{u}^{2}\n$$\n\ndefines a parabola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/8b/8b35fbafaf8d121bceef7a0763253df075fe4fe85eb3c992f95c59d05f986c67.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389195 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2407dcd5-06ff-568b-bb6d-b2494e2bd942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389197} {:answer/id #uuid "07712d2e-2b92-521c-b6ae-d43d00f464d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/13/1368ee41963c1abcc320d2cd35449c08599582f37c7d68f750e137fcdcd2e57b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389198} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3ba601-6388-5b1c-ac01-5522063f4425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fd/fd2a51ca3a1654c2e857018268bf251366472a2c5d7f90009d5ccfbf1b1edd9d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389199} {:answer/id #uuid "dc797ebb-78cb-5a5b-ac03-97f75fe59dce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389200} {:answer/id #uuid "2201cc6c-a008-5122-982f-2699a2c582b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/95/95bf5e059fda0fc1ba8423b35fffa57d9cae0cc2a2a7e40b0c2539b6a7ae7678.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389201}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389197} :answer-field/id #uuid "644861a4-b1bb-5e40-b887-ad0f73e8386a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "82d50c5e-c224-54bc-84fa-f5633f671803" :question/math-academy-id "q-161840" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+{v}^{2} \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2v \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (2v) \\\\  & =2u+2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u+2v & <0 \\\\ 2v & <-2u \\\\ v & <-u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=-u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(-1,-1)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n![](images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44718c67-0ba1-524e-9c98-36d26b5e3a27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389204} {:answer/id #uuid "225446d3-d261-5650-bed9-eaacbb9f2ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/8b/8bec16b8ed5083071e21761e6b2e649e136ce0662f7e5ab764161491094c0609.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389205} {:answer/id #uuid "f7224ff9-23ce-5fb3-a58f-396aece4ce79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fd/fd2a51ca3a1654c2e857018268bf251366472a2c5d7f90009d5ccfbf1b1edd9d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389206} {:answer/id #uuid "133f4c43-8534-5694-8789-da248d6d37e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389207} {:answer/id #uuid "83b86f4d-ddaa-5fd0-acc9-387f47e13895" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/0b/0b764651dbc7999e7af13214685796b5045fab89f101bbc72bf413112df3c51f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389208}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389205} :answer-field/id #uuid "10f005f2-f2a3-59bb-859f-a90699b333ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a4aa2b6c-6ba5-59e1-9b2a-ad9aa14c6d6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-161841" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ -uv\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-preserving?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving at the points where the Jacobian determinant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}>0.\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ -uv\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ -v & -u\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot (-u)-(-v)\\cdot {e}^{u+v} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}(v-u).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{u+v}(v-u) & >0 \\\\ v-u & >0 \\\\ v & >u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=u\n$$\n\ndefines a straight line, and the coordinates of, say, $(2,3)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-preserving must be the following:\n\n![](images/8b/8bec16b8ed5083071e21761e6b2e649e136ce0662f7e5ab764161491094c0609.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72f242ce-c65f-5c89-b0bc-7d65d210918f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c2/c244350524a52123e2e97db4cd8eb4f57737b8b15746eb72a8d54eac32a4f3cf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389211} {:answer/id #uuid "62f79a35-0524-561a-ba91-d11cf1227837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/4d/4d944eb13437b86c0cf6ae4fa8bca09031ed0f2f85a9f20bcb0050a6de9f8ebb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389212} {:answer/id #uuid "cfa99b0c-e94e-5958-a986-2c13e020da52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389213} {:answer/id #uuid "acff9e64-428c-54e8-8971-9f328584d347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fe/fe7bb422a3f992ea88a900e63b2c55df99bce3a785929901c248abadfadf23f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389214} {:answer/id #uuid "2b75873a-335e-5bb0-9312-2f0d35d3633e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f7/f7bc22ac4dfd2d7015952144ece63f91ecccef2a992649aece60dc8a70f1d018.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389215}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389213} :answer-field/id #uuid "317c3736-80dd-5537-ba65-91575de09aa0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389210}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e552dcc6-87b8-5585-84f4-2c455a9099f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161856" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the region in the $uv$-plane where the transformation $T,$ given by \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ -2u-{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nis (locally) orientation-reversing?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing at the points where the Jacobian determinant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}<0\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{2}+2v \\\\ -2u-{v}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2u & 2 \\\\ -2 & -2v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2u\\cdot (-2v)-(-2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4uv+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the required regions, we solve the following inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4uv+4 & <0 \\\\ -4uv & <-4 \\\\ uv & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis gives the solution $v>\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u>0$ and $v<\\frac{1}{u}$ for $u<0.$\n\nNotice that the equation \n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{u}\n$$\n\ndefines a hyperbola, and the coordinates of, say, $(1,2)$ and $(-1,-2)$ satisfy the inequality. \n\nTherefore, the region where the transformation is (locally) orientation-reversing must be the following:\n\n   \n\n![](images/d4/d477a9f312fc2fb0a5a4c2d13164c96118aa70b53cf23db80be9ec698151e6fd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389216 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efa02fb0-8bd7-58b2-846f-b834fa4d00a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389218} {:answer/id #uuid "f26a101a-d1b7-54cb-9da6-045b68d673b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389219} {:answer/id #uuid "102a5894-9256-52e2-86ff-598cf595625d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389220} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e6000c-9905-5a76-9098-325dd8f54c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389221} {:answer/id #uuid "eca4c4f3-4d0e-5221-a6c4-b18b4d27e0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "17\\,\\delta A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389222}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389221} :answer-field/id #uuid "c652ad8c-b6bc-516a-b0a9-9d72286277e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389217}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dc1b537d-1bb4-5840-be74-781a73ccdf95" :question/math-academy-id "q-161876" :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(-2,2)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{u+v} \\\\ {u}^{3}+v\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{u+v} & {e}^{u+v} \\\\ 3{u}^{2} & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}\\cdot 1-3{u}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{u+v} \\\\  & ={e}^{u+v}(1-3{u}^{2}) \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(-2,2)} & ={e}^{u+v}(1-3{u}^{2})\\,{|}_{u=-2\\,v=2} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2+2}(1-3(-2)^{2}) \\\\  & =-11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|-11|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =11\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389319 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b30b0f5f-97bd-583a-a307-e3397fb59a93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=(-2y+2)\\,i-2x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389321} {:answer/id #uuid "329fb401-7f68-5ec6-8f25-98f6ed849d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=(2y-2)\\,i-4x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389322} {:answer/id #uuid "8f39913b-20f7-5b49-bfad-b41649dde17e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=2xy\\,i-2x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389323} {:answer/id #uuid "0a193adf-d260-5b1e-b6a6-38f14c829c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=y\\,i+2x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389324} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce39ff5-c2b6-5f45-bc52-6fcb1efcfc46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=-2y\\,i-(2x-2)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389325}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389325} :answer-field/id #uuid "db983459-2780-56ca-9561-aa9eaaa313f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389320}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "44bb9bca-3844-5057-9f71-7974dba4c933" :question/math-academy-id "q-162504" :question/problem "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x-{y}^{2}+1.$" :question/worked-solution "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-2y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-2x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}-2x-{y}^{2}+1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =-2y\\,i-(2x-2)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389326 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "650f7b08-bfd6-5c2c-bec5-476c49332af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=(5x{y}^{2}-xy)\\,i-(5{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}-y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389328} {:answer/id #uuid "914cb7f4-06c3-5620-8b1f-a5e8ed515d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=(15x{y}^{2}-x)\\,i-(5{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389329} {:answer/id #uuid "c1f8a410-e48f-5314-b3a2-9ef6d48ff12e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=(15{y}^{2}-x)\\,i-(5{y}^{3}+3{x}^{2})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389330} {:answer/id #uuid "70c29385-9850-5254-9ec6-162ae3af2506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=5x{y}^{2}\\,i-(5{y}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389331} {:answer/id #uuid "e7fe5b53-4ca1-54d7-9895-0e3672329dbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=(5x{y}^{2}-x)\\,i-(5{y}^{3}-y)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389332}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389329} :answer-field/id #uuid "9692a0d2-3cc0-58cd-84e1-4dba68de9887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "acd27a04-e10f-58dd-b26e-c84cd03b0423" :question/math-academy-id "q-162512" :question/problem "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $ψ(x,y)=5{y}^{3}x-{x}^{3}-xy.$" :question/worked-solution "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5{y}^{3}x-{x}^{3}-xy) \\\\  & =15x{y}^{2}-x, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5{y}^{3}x-{x}^{3}-xy) \\\\  & =-(5{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)=5{y}^{3}x-{x}^{3}-xy$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =(15x{y}^{2}-x)\\,i-(5{y}^{3}-3{x}^{2}-y)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389339 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc9bdb8d-ad2b-5d91-86ca-825ec11037fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/6a/6a1ce9b96f0bca4ec14e7ce60e5cebd72efc4421acfd4a65ba55e9c8a7d1d352.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389341} {:answer/id #uuid "678a4e30-3832-553e-8ea1-b4ab930f1cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/07/0799c425e6b33f2be8f115d7d0de9e5607bdef9f094a02381fc5197839d80d75.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389342} {:answer/id #uuid "6db4de8e-dbc7-51ee-8ae9-5b089f44fbf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/95/95fee99b51dc07130717569fcb90ab5e210bf2cc64811f536dc493e9c3e1588e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389343} {:answer/id #uuid "92bd4bba-9b1d-550f-a3d8-850c5ca8fd4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/3a/3a3ef451c7e25ff33fb1820d5b8c98e10038c4ee058f627d93acf72989b7b794.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389344} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc3c2dd-f56c-5123-bcfe-941b923629fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/fb/fb7119466ac4f884fca55cfb3fc0b487703249050211ff922549a702a047bb39.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389345}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389344} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce55e1ed-b422-5201-909f-65fba9887b50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389340}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6b93df85-0b3c-5ded-8201-a97d95285f56" :question/math-academy-id "q-162629" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $F(x,y)=2xi-2y\\,j$ over the domain $D={R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2xi-2y\\,j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=2x$ and $Q(x,y)=-2y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2-2=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=2y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=2xy+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=2xy+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=2xy.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)=2xy$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ 2xy & =c \\\\ y & =\\frac{c}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=0$ and $x=0$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=0$ and $x=0.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/24/24c6dadd4f980121a36929a9cdb800f7da8fac8eb84af40d595af89aa77b11ea.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389346 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9a4a14-a956-5391-ac3a-4943156bef2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/32/3296bdd0ae6e92949ee3728e01592f1758917cf03446e75317af341ef669f606.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389348} {:answer/id #uuid "abbaeebd-0469-5172-b7c3-8cb76cac7c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/c9/c9ba45e905fc98c5011727c483ba444088c81a0423cc85912af24e1e8c19f1cc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389349} {:answer/id #uuid "e67f9b71-0c0d-5761-ba3f-d60318d3579b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/75/7585305cff504d86c4b910455acb21036503bdc0073989ca8351a751f1fea9b8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389350} {:answer/id #uuid "a8aac5dd-ff50-5af5-a307-0a9ee9b5c0d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/ff/ff6709c054d8c1e3cd21bbe87e7326f802471fa321a90346e3f173f72b443896.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389351} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff7114c-d67e-5f98-a63a-d5f5e37f4d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/f6/f6926d3f6d26fe712b53e53c06af440575dbe858e373cfae5d7d4b5017f44b93.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389352}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389349} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9db8e2f-b628-5684-b658-6125f7acf877" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bc28542a-a43a-5f01-b243-0063900eefa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-162630" :question/problem "Which of the following represents the streamlines of the vector field $F(x,y)=2y\\,i+2x\\,j$ over the domain $D={R}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The streamlines of a vector field are the level curves of its stream function and are tangential to the vector field at every point. \n\nIf $F$ represents the velocity field of a fluid flow, then they show the path traced out by a massless particle as it moves with the flow.\n\nA vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that \n\n$$\nF(x,y)=2y\\,i+2x\\,j\n$$\n\n is a vector field with simply connected domain $D,$ where $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=-2x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=-{x}^{2}+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f(x)=-{x}^{2}$ or $g(y)={y}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the level curves of the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}$ given by the equations $ψ(x,y)=c.$\n\nNotice that the range of $ψ(x,y)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, we will consider level curves for any value of $c.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ψ(x,y) & =c \\\\ {y}^{2}-{x}^{2} & =c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above gives us a family of hyperbolas with asymptotes $y=x$ and $x=-x$ and, for $c=0,$ the lines $y=x$ and $y=-x.$ Therefore, the correct graph (along with the vector field $F$) is as follows:\n\n![](images/9b/9b0d2f98a0b71beb773b802908f01691924492a5f3abff3db75be4a0ae458e7d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f817dec0-31ee-5a90-a6ae-2391b9cf9403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨3,2⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393210} {:answer/id #uuid "d27aae8e-1b41-5a3d-9baf-285744999d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨4,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393211} {:answer/id #uuid "1898f2e5-1406-5cc8-82d7-46e5635ddabe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨-5,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393212} {:answer/id #uuid "4d956027-e0b5-5ae7-a8b7-35ca7afa539e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393213} {:answer/id #uuid "9b764f7f-086e-5989-aeae-04810e887220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "⟨5,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393214}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393214} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2b7a822-706f-59e1-aaeb-ea5e1255d429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393209}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "85c3a288-e595-578f-a534-db75ed9d872f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72377" :question/problem "Given the function $f(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}+xy+2x$, find the equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,1).$" :question/worked-solution "The equation of the tangent line to the level curve $f(x,y)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}+xy+2x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}+xy+2x)\\,j \\\\  & =(2{y}^{2}+y+2)\\,i+(4xy+x)\\,j \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1) & =(2(1)^{2}+(1)+2)\\,i+(4(1)(1)+(1))\\,j \\\\  & =5\\,i+5\\,j \\\\  & =⟨5,5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the Cartesian equation of the tangent line to the level curve that passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨5,5⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f357254-f769-5436-bd0b-25358181a0f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/d0/d0abe9cb42672156fe48a934e1c9337af315d16d8afd75c8092ca9a01657e922.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395516} {:answer/id #uuid "bed2a6a9-752e-5e4f-8000-ecbcb240a187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/12/12c8be4491fe81d8ccf4ea964d883f2dc079f5183b72a8cd5b38260b8d3229d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395517} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c05222-2416-5b40-9ab2-a005052e8eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a1/a1e81bf264c71d1e4b14ea7a4f845c8276626e49238e10275afd9a1eeee22eec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395518} {:answer/id #uuid "0447b677-359b-5b6f-a358-d3e5ef69c66d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/b5/b54f31e2780f3f6c11ecc69eaab5dee59ddf2ba0c542f1e9bbee4f8fe25284ea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395519} {:answer/id #uuid "bfabcff4-75db-540e-9198-01c700bc07c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497} :answer/value "images/a6/a6b83ae381ebc092e930f588b7ccd0851d09b521664285d700157c7091794fc2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395520}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395517} :answer-field/id #uuid "d30dfba9-dad9-5198-8051-754b14711f5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "82dba76d-c396-5269-a0c3-fa0f434c28a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-98398" :question/problem "Which of the following regions is *not* connected?" :question/worked-solution "A region $D$ is connected if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path in $D.$ \n\nAmong the given options, the only one that does not satisfy this definition is the following:\n\n  \n\n![](images/12/12c8be4491fe81d8ccf4ea964d883f2dc079f5183b72a8cd5b38260b8d3229d5.png)\n\nNote that if we take two points $P$ and $Q$, as shown below, there is no path lying entirely within the region that connects them.\n\n   \n\n![](images/b3/b3dd1c29d770f36e4542427175eab7e102ee3e43a944d525459d28257e6d24a7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3c6467b-917f-5aae-9cc9-3d8de87bf705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395847} {:answer/id #uuid "95acc209-e214-5661-af27-7731166005f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{3}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395848} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0c76fb-2820-5cf1-9645-b9dbc230a965" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(1,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395849} {:answer/id #uuid "bef1175c-5920-532e-b7b5-9da5b087ef6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395850} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5d5b3b-9e1f-57a7-8376-c15ae99d31d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395851}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395851} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ad0dc77-76f9-5eaa-aa66-ed1f16aa44f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7795ea1b-86ec-5a3d-baba-dbdd67574697" :question/math-academy-id "q-36911" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+2(y-3)^{2}$, find the points $(x,y)$ where $∇f(x,y)=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1)+2(y-3)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2(y-3)^{2}) \\\\  & =0+2\\cdot 2(y-3) \\\\  & =4(y-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+4(y-3)\\,j.$\n\nNow, we solve $∇f(x,y)=0:$ \n\n$$\n\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,i+4(y-3)\\,j=0\\text{ }i+0\\text{ }j\n$$\n\nSo, by equating the coefficients of $i$ and $j,$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} &=0 \\\\ 4(y-3) &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $x=0$ and from the second equation, we obtain $y=3.$ \n\nTherefore, $∇f(x,y)=0$ at the point $(0,3).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396120 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2368068-c473-5919-aff6-53a14b246b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396122} {:answer/id #uuid "801b88de-1112-5c33-bd1b-ddb864de2c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396123} {:answer/id #uuid "dcd49079-798b-58f7-8490-cd6fb613a033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396124} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b72ba2-9df1-5acc-b385-173c4e36f9a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$18$ and $9$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396125} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6ef04e-258f-5d68-a139-8ed8a39c2ddc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6\\sqrt{2}$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396126}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396126} :answer-field/id #uuid "bef4ba21-7e9c-5949-8c09-0a45d37f8298" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "480a616c-8c80-5f81-b3d5-aa7488c0665b" :question/math-academy-id "q-7596" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{18}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=9$ and ${b}^{2}=18.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=9,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=18,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=3\\sqrt{2},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}=6\\sqrt{2},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 3=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396127 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dcb6c4d2-fc5f-560f-9373-4f7a33529527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4\\sqrt{5}$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396129} {:answer/id #uuid "6565345d-7cca-5723-8a4a-06b781ec17a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2\\sqrt{5}$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396130} {:answer/id #uuid "71576bd8-08e5-58e9-999c-df3860cd2885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$20$ and $16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396131} {:answer/id #uuid "b48bdeab-9afb-508b-a31f-840d2716535b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$10$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396132} {:answer/id #uuid "7fd4bf3f-6eb6-53a8-9729-d9955e874477" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{5}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396133}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396129} :answer-field/id #uuid "abca09ba-0b0a-5885-90fb-fd1d7d5af56c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e572cb8d-0721-59ec-897a-5e0fa685feb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-7597" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{20}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=20$ and ${b}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=20,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{5},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}=4\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 4=8.$"}]}
